1
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:09,766
Rym: It's Tuesday, November 26th, 2024.

2
00:00:09,766 --> 00:00:11,271
Rym: I'm Rym, and this is GeekNights.

3
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:16,559
Rym: Tonight, we're talking about Obelus.

4
00:00:17,662 --> 00:00:18,698
Scott: Let's do this!

5
00:00:20,341 --> 00:00:29,139
Scott: All right, so we've forgotten everything that happened during our period of no episodes, and now we gotta talk about more recent things that we actually remember with our goldfish brains.

6
00:00:30,741 --> 00:00:52,163
Scott: Uh, so, legendary person Frank Oz, if people don't know, for some- I don't know how you would be a nerd and not know who Frank Oz is, but Frank Oz is a voice actor and puppeteer of Miss Piggy, Fozzie the Bear, Animal, Cookie Monster, Bert, Yoda.

7
00:00:52,624 --> 00:00:53,687
Scott: Those are all the same person.

8
00:00:54,489 --> 00:00:55,873
Scott: That's this person, right?

9
00:00:56,354 --> 00:01:05,191
Scott: That alone would already make you a legendary figure, worldwide historic figure, right?

10
00:01:06,013 --> 00:01:15,800
Scott: People may not know, Frank Oz also has acted just as a live-action actor, sometimes in small parts, like he was in the Blues Brothers, did you know that, Rym?

11
00:01:16,164 --> 00:01:17,179
Rym: I did not know that.

12
00:01:17,882 --> 00:01:19,239
Scott: He's the prison guy.

13
00:01:20,621 --> 00:01:21,198
Rym: Goddamn!

14
00:01:24,766 --> 00:01:26,880
Scott: He is also- Prophylactic soiled.

15
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:29,216
Scott: Exactly.

16
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:30,335
Scott: That's him.

17
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:33,100
Scott: Same guy, even next time you watch Blues Brothers.

18
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:34,900
Scott: Prophylactic soiled.

19
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:39,180
Rym: Also, he did the voice of Elwood's building manager, who you don't see on screen.

20
00:01:40,021 --> 00:01:40,819
Scott: Okay, I didn't know that.

21
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:41,700
Scott: But it doesn't matter.

22
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:45,560
Scott: He has also directed a whole bunch of movies.

23
00:01:45,860 --> 00:01:49,758
Scott: Some of them expected, like the Dark Crystal or the Muppets.

24
00:01:49,778 --> 00:01:50,160
Scott: Take Manhattan.

25
00:01:50,660 --> 00:01:51,563
Scott: Imagine Muppets.

26
00:01:51,583 --> 00:01:52,567
Scott: Take Manhattan Dark Crystal.

27
00:01:52,607 --> 00:01:56,039
Scott: He's puppeting in the movie, but he's also directing the movie.

28
00:01:57,520 --> 00:01:59,095
Scott: Little Shop of Horrors directed that.

29
00:02:01,323 --> 00:02:01,740
Scott: Bowfinger.

30
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:03,739
Rym: Wait, what about Bob, huh?

31
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:04,956
Rym: Yep, he directed that.

32
00:02:06,502 --> 00:02:10,560
Rym: I've not seen What About Bob since the 90s, but I remember it being surprisingly good.

33
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:13,040
Scott: Yeah, it's a legendary dude.

34
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:27,040
Scott: And so, at the Museum of the Moving Image, which I can walk to from my apartment and I have a membership at, I assume he lives somewhere nearby because there's a whole bunch of screenings of all his movies over there.

35
00:02:28,201 --> 00:02:30,980
Scott: And he just keeps showing up for Q&As, right?

36
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:32,239
Scott: On a lot of them.

37
00:02:33,102 --> 00:02:39,640
Scott: They put up a screening and then a couple days later they're like, "Oh, yeah, Frank decided he'll come over for this one also, by the

38
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:39,760
Scott: way.".

39
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:43,619
Scott: We didn't originally list it as he would be appearing for a Q&A, but he is.

40
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:45,539
Scott: He just keeps coming over there, right?

41
00:02:45,620 --> 00:02:47,240
Scott: Which is kinda crazy.

42
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:51,500
Scott: Because usually legendary people of that stature, right?

43
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:53,959
Scott: They might do a Q&A like one time.

44
00:02:55,204 --> 00:02:57,220
Scott: Or they're very limited and restricted.

45
00:02:59,544 --> 00:03:00,759
Scott: This dude, we didn't wait to see.

46
00:03:01,782 --> 00:03:04,600
Scott: I wasn't able to go to a bunch of the screenings because I was doing shit.

47
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:07,779
Scott: But I went to the one for Death at a Funeral, which I hadn't seen.

48
00:03:09,843 --> 00:03:13,160
Scott: I guess I knew there was a movie with that name and I didn't really know anything about it.

49
00:03:13,282 --> 00:03:14,460
Rym: I've also never seen that movie.

50
00:03:15,032 --> 00:03:16,000
Scott: You should see that movie.

51
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:19,519
Scott: Just to see the acting of Alan Tudyk, if nothing else.

52
00:03:20,861 --> 00:03:27,880
Scott: Alan Tudyk plays a dude who takes a hallucinogenic drug and is tripping for the majority of the film.

53
00:03:28,841 --> 00:03:31,840
Scott: And to be able to act as a person.

54
00:03:32,322 --> 00:03:34,379
Scott: He's not acting as a person who's hallucinating.

55
00:03:34,921 --> 00:03:36,953
Scott: Obviously he didn't use drugs for real.

56
00:03:39,282 --> 00:03:42,859
Scott: He's acting as a person at a funeral who knows he's taken drugs.

57
00:03:44,546 --> 00:03:46,480
Scott: And is trying to keep it together.

58
00:03:47,784 --> 00:03:52,139
Scott: But has taken a very strong hallucinogen at the same time.

59
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:56,920
Scott: And so that alone is worth seeing the movie.

60
00:03:57,341 --> 00:03:59,600
Scott: But the rest of the movie is also hilarious.

61
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:02,660
Scott: But this dude does something I've never seen before.

62
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:05,760
Scott: Which is A, he shows up and watches the movie.

63
00:04:07,361 --> 00:04:11,600
Scott: Most of these Q&A people show up when the movie's over and then they leave.

64
00:04:11,902 --> 00:04:14,180
Rym: They roll in, maybe they sit in a green room for 10 minutes.

65
00:04:15,867 --> 00:04:17,498
Scott: I saw him just walk in and I'm like, "Is that

66
00:04:17,539 --> 00:04:17,639
Scott: him?".

67
00:04:17,740 --> 00:04:19,480
Scott: And he just sits down and watches the movie.

68
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:23,120
Scott: And then at the end of the Q&A, sure enough, he stands up and just sits on the thing.

69
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:30,460
Rym: Think about how you would be if you were notable enough to where if you went to any institution that was related to your stuff.

70
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:32,156
Rym: And was like, "Hey, you want me to do a

71
00:04:32,176 --> 00:04:32,378
Rym: Q&A?".

72
00:04:32,620 --> 00:04:33,468
Rym: They would be like, "Oh, sir,

73
00:04:33,529 --> 00:04:33,812
Rym: please.".

74
00:04:34,861 --> 00:04:37,240
Scott: And then when the Q&A actually comes.

75
00:04:37,521 --> 00:04:39,117
Scott: This dude is 80 years old, I think.

76
00:04:40,443 --> 00:04:43,279
Scott: Dude has a better memory than either of us.

77
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:46,880
Scott: This movie he made in 2007.

78
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:50,800
Scott: So what can you remember about a GeekNights you did in 2007?

79
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:50,890
Scott: Nine.

80
00:04:52,460 --> 00:04:52,571
Scott: Right?

81
00:04:53,822 --> 00:04:59,741
Rym: I did do commentary on old GeekNights from 2005.

82
00:04:59,741 --> 00:05:03,980
Scott: This guy remembered all the details of making this movie.

83
00:05:04,761 --> 00:05:08,360
Scott: How they made the set, how he casted the people.

84
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:15,900
Scott: He remembered specific direction he gave to people in specific scenes to get them to perform in a certain way.

85
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:21,100
Rym: I remember the production of the Katsucon Late Show in excruciating detail.

86
00:05:22,244 --> 00:05:22,820
Scott: Sure, okay.

87
00:05:22,982 --> 00:05:23,739
Scott: I remember a little bit.

88
00:05:26,183 --> 00:05:28,820
Rym: You fell asleep on stage while Steve Bennett was talking about his hat.

89
00:05:29,900 --> 00:05:29,988
Scott: Yeah.

90
00:05:30,981 --> 00:05:34,440
Scott: This is the kind of guy where the moderator guy would ask one question.

91
00:05:34,902 --> 00:05:36,799
Scott: And he would just go off, right?

92
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:37,839
Scott: He was talking a whole bunch.

93
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:42,300
Scott: But even though he talked a whole bunch, he didn't stray off topic, right?

94
00:05:42,340 --> 00:05:47,280
Scott: He was answering the question in incredible, fascinating detail.

95
00:05:49,922 --> 00:05:54,540
Scott: And so just to even be in the same room as a legendary person like that.

96
00:05:56,160 --> 00:05:57,480
Scott: He's going to be there again.

97
00:05:57,901 --> 00:06:00,680
Scott: They're showing Empire Strikes Back on Saturday.

98
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:03,860
Scott: Which I saw, but he wasn't listed as showing up for that.

99
00:06:03,940 --> 00:06:05,412
Scott: But then they edit it and they're like, "You know what?

100
00:06:05,554 --> 00:06:06,179
Scott: He is showing

101
00:06:06,259 --> 00:06:06,300
Scott: up.".

102
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:09,917
Scott: And it's like, "Alright, well I guess I'll go watch the crappy special

103
00:06:09,978 --> 00:06:10,319
Scott: edition.".

104
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:13,074
Scott: Because you really don't want to just show up for the Q&A only.

105
00:06:13,094 --> 00:06:14,200
Scott: That's kind of weird, right?

106
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:16,600
Rym: I haven't seen that movie cover to cover in a while.

107
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:20,000
Scott: I've seen it probably, I don't even know how many times.

108
00:06:20,140 --> 00:06:20,541
Scott: You know what?

109
00:06:20,802 --> 00:06:23,208
Scott: I can't remember Geek Dice in 2007.

110
00:06:23,208 --> 00:06:27,860
Scott: I can probably write down the script for the Empire Strikes Back.

111
00:06:29,980 --> 00:06:30,885
Scott: Right, okay.

112
00:06:30,926 --> 00:06:31,710
Scott: So, yeah.

113
00:06:34,842 --> 00:06:35,383
Scott: That's something.

114
00:06:35,484 --> 00:06:39,635
Scott: If you're in New York City, or wherever you are in the world.

115
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:46,000
Scott: If you have a chance to be in the same room as Frank Oz, you should take yourself up.

116
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:48,660
Rym: Yeah, Saturday 3pm at the Redstone Theatre.

117
00:06:49,281 --> 00:06:50,257
Scott: Yeah, don't pass it up.

118
00:06:52,362 --> 00:06:55,900
Scott: If for some reason you didn't know who Frank Oz was, what the hell?

119
00:06:55,961 --> 00:06:57,840
Rym: Well, you're about to find out, hopefully.

120
00:06:58,604 --> 00:06:58,976
Rym: I don't know.

121
00:06:59,120 --> 00:07:00,120
Rym: Just go read his Wikipedia.

122
00:07:01,540 --> 00:07:03,880
Scott: A deserving legendary person.

123
00:07:05,282 --> 00:07:08,559
Scott: If I end up like that kind of old man, that would be terrific.

124
00:07:09,660 --> 00:07:10,619
Rym: So, in some news.

125
00:07:11,160 --> 00:07:19,540
Rym: So if you're not a magic person, you may not be aware that of all the ways to play magic, there's a way that is very popular called Commander.

126
00:07:20,220 --> 00:07:22,912
Scott: Yeah, this is where you put out your... I don't know the details.

127
00:07:22,953 --> 00:07:24,540
Scott: I've never played Commander, to my knowledge.

128
00:07:24,601 --> 00:07:25,920
Rym: I know vaguely the details.

129
00:07:27,242 --> 00:07:30,140
Scott: You're like a planeswalker, and you put it out at the start of the game, right?

130
00:07:30,282 --> 00:07:32,142
Rym: Unlike old... Now, I don't know.

131
00:07:32,464 --> 00:07:34,372
Rym: Magic's changed a lot since 1993, when I played Magic a lot.

132
00:07:37,343 --> 00:07:39,960
Rym: But, you know, it used to be there's a minimum deck size.

133
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:42,400
Rym: Commander, there's just a deck size.

134
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:43,707
Rym: 100 cards.

135
00:07:44,010 --> 00:07:45,360
Rym: No more, no less.

136
00:07:46,221 --> 00:07:48,359
Rym: Two, you can only have one of any card.

137
00:07:48,562 --> 00:07:49,440
Rym: No other than lands.

138
00:07:49,560 --> 00:07:51,440
Rym: No four of a card, it's just one.

139
00:07:52,061 --> 00:07:53,259
Rym: You can use almost any card.

140
00:07:53,541 --> 00:07:57,240
Rym: There's very little that's banned, but one of anything, no matter what.

141
00:07:57,680 --> 00:08:01,900
Rym: So you gotta fill out a deck of 100 cards with only one of any one given cards.

142
00:08:01,980 --> 00:08:03,639
Rym: There's a lot of randomness in there.

143
00:08:04,020 --> 00:08:05,938
Rym: Like, your deck can't be that well-tuned.

144
00:08:06,540 --> 00:08:08,980
Rym: But also, you gotta pick your commander.

145
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:13,800
Rym: And basically, the way it works is you pick one of those legendary-type creatures.

146
00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:15,433
Rym: Like, you know the ones in the old...

147
00:08:16,600 --> 00:08:18,260
Scott: The planeswalkers, that's what I was talking about.

148
00:08:18,260 --> 00:08:18,351
Scott: Yeah.

149
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:21,059
Scott: Some really strong commander character.

150
00:08:21,741 --> 00:08:23,920
Rym: Where these come from is in the old days.

151
00:08:24,101 --> 00:08:25,480
Rym: Now, this is really old man talking.

152
00:08:25,580 --> 00:08:28,380
Rym: None of you played Magic when, like, Legends was out.

153
00:08:28,762 --> 00:08:31,260
Rym: But in Legends, that introduced gold cards.

154
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:36,880
Rym: And gold cards were the cards that had, like, a bunch of different mana types.

155
00:08:36,980 --> 00:08:40,640
Rym: Like, yeah, here's a 2020 dragon that kicks ass, but guess what?

156
00:08:40,981 --> 00:08:42,779
Rym: You need, like, four different fucking colors to play.

157
00:08:43,201 --> 00:08:46,940
Scott: The original name of commander mode was Elder Dragon Highlander.

158
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:51,240
Scott: Which, you used those Elder Dragon cards from the Highlander set.

159
00:08:51,700 --> 00:08:52,739
Scott: From the Legends set.

160
00:08:52,820 --> 00:08:54,720
Rym: Yeah, because there was really no other way to play those cards.

161
00:08:54,860 --> 00:08:57,840
Rym: Like, there was no universe where those would naturally come out and play.

162
00:08:58,980 --> 00:08:59,080
Scott: Yeah.

163
00:09:02,721 --> 00:09:07,360
Scott: And you've seen, like, these mechanics from commander have been borrowed in other games.

164
00:09:07,580 --> 00:09:09,920
Scott: Like, Netrunner, a lot of Star Wars card games.

165
00:09:10,020 --> 00:09:14,440
Scott: A lot of these card games, you have a character that has some, like, base set of abilities.

166
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:15,900
Scott: Hearthstone, right?

167
00:09:16,241 --> 00:09:20,760
Scott: That you sort of put out at the start of the game as, like, this is who I am, right?

168
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:22,660
Scott: This is my special base ability.

169
00:09:22,861 --> 00:09:26,700
Scott: Like, you're playing, you know, a board game like Dune where you have a character or a fighting game.

170
00:09:27,221 --> 00:09:31,600
Scott: And then you build your deck to complement your main character's ability.

171
00:09:31,981 --> 00:09:35,299
Scott: So, you know, commander, this, you know, mode someone came up with.

172
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:42,480
Scott: Other card games have then borrowed from that core mechanic and reused it because it's a good idea.

173
00:09:43,142 --> 00:09:48,300
Rym: And it really evolved from the old Star format that I'm pretty sure has been dead for multiple decades.

174
00:09:48,740 --> 00:09:52,380
Rym: That was, like, the five player version of Magic that was semi-official.

175
00:09:52,580 --> 00:09:56,300
Rym: Might have even been published official rules a long time ago, but it's dead.

176
00:09:56,520 --> 00:09:57,318
Rym: It wasn't even that good.

177
00:09:57,862 --> 00:09:59,099
Rym: But it does a lot of clever things.

178
00:09:59,440 --> 00:10:04,259
Rym: Like, your commander sits in a special area and you can play them like a card into the board at any time.

179
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:06,059
Rym: Like, as a creature and do their thing.

180
00:10:06,543 --> 00:10:08,240
Rym: Or just keep them, like, out of the fray.

181
00:10:09,305 --> 00:10:10,180
Rym: And it's a free-for-all.

182
00:10:10,302 --> 00:10:11,299
Rym: Like, three to five players.

183
00:10:11,760 --> 00:10:16,020
Rym: So, the politics of "I just attacked Scott" totally come into play.

184
00:10:16,901 --> 00:10:22,960
Rym: And it seems like I've never played commander, but it seems like it is really fun.

185
00:10:23,462 --> 00:10:27,540
Rym: And it seems like the community, broadly, really likes to play commander.

186
00:10:28,360 --> 00:10:28,661
Scott: Of course.

187
00:10:28,702 --> 00:10:33,120
Scott: I mean, it's a way that somebody can play and enjoy the fun parts of Magic the Gathering.

188
00:10:33,781 --> 00:10:36,360
Scott: Without spending a fortune, right?

189
00:10:36,540 --> 00:10:39,800
Scott: To get, like, four copies of every insane powerful card.

190
00:10:40,820 --> 00:10:50,378
Scott: And then you get to... There's a lot less of, "Oh, that guy just beat me because he's super competitive and made a streamlined

191
00:10:50,399 --> 00:10:50,599
Scott: deck.".

192
00:10:51,061 --> 00:10:55,100
Scott: And also, a lot of cards that you would never play in normal Magic.

193
00:10:55,340 --> 00:10:59,180
Scott: You're sort of forced to play with more varied cards.

194
00:10:59,862 --> 00:11:01,580
Scott: And you get different hands each time.

195
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:06,600
Scott: So, if you look at, say, Marvel Snap, where you have Arashem, which fills your deck with a bunch of random cards.

196
00:11:07,581 --> 00:11:11,040
Scott: That's the same kind of fun element that commander has.

197
00:11:12,203 --> 00:11:18,160
Scott: Where it's like, yes, you still get to keep the element of self-expression in deck building.

198
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:21,040
Scott: Because you're still choosing a hundred cards and choosing a commander.

199
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:25,880
Scott: And so, your personality makes it into your playstyle.

200
00:11:26,246 --> 00:11:26,939
Scott: It gets shown.

201
00:11:27,741 --> 00:11:32,400
Scott: But, there's also sort of wild, wild elements, right?

202
00:11:32,981 --> 00:11:37,420
Scott: To not the kind of randomness where it's like you win and lose based on luck, although there is some of that.

203
00:11:37,580 --> 00:11:43,100
Scott: But the kind of random elements where there's, you know, surprise and discovery that occurs.

204
00:11:43,400 --> 00:11:50,700
Rym: However, in this format, despite having a hundred cards, over time there were a handful of cards.

205
00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:53,359
Rym: Two in particular, but really there's like four or five.

206
00:11:53,780 --> 00:11:54,669
Scott: Two recent ones.

207
00:11:56,080 --> 00:12:03,040
Rym: But, these cards, despite hundred card decks, basically had to be in your deck if you wanted to win.

208
00:12:03,240 --> 00:12:05,480
Rym: Like, they were increasingly dominating the meta.

209
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:07,526
Rym: You kind of needed them.

210
00:12:07,587 --> 00:12:09,620
Rym: Like, they're just great in this format.

211
00:12:10,720 --> 00:12:14,660
Scott: Your odds of drawing them are low, because this one in a hundred, right?

212
00:12:14,740 --> 00:12:18,440
Scott: You might not ever draw it unless you have another card that helps you draw more, which you probably do.

213
00:12:18,680 --> 00:12:19,839
Scott: But, you gotta draw that.

214
00:12:20,140 --> 00:12:21,729
Rym: I wonder if I can use demonic tutor in commander.

215
00:12:24,061 --> 00:12:31,120
Scott: I don't know, but the point is that these cards are going to be one of your few of your hundred if they're allowed.

216
00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:39,800
Scott: And so, in order to play commander, even though it's not a super competitive mode, you know, all the time, you're going to want those cards in there.

217
00:12:39,940 --> 00:12:45,120
Scott: And so, everyone who plays commander, it's like, yeah, they got ninety other cards to pick from their own.

218
00:12:45,180 --> 00:12:46,574
Scott: But, like, these ones you need.

219
00:12:47,381 --> 00:12:49,437
Scott: And so, the prices of those cards went up.

220
