Scott: I feel like they're not rich.

412
00:28:26,070 --> 00:28:26,867
Scott: They're trying to get rich.

413
00:28:27,350 --> 00:28:28,950
Rym: Yeah, I guess they're trying to get rich.

414
00:28:29,330 --> 00:28:33,038
Rym: This is not a Scrooge McDuck type of endeavor.

415
00:28:33,119 --> 00:28:34,990
Rym: This is a bunch of Glomgold's adventure.

416
00:28:37,075 --> 00:28:39,036
Rym: I guess he was also rich, technically.

417
00:28:39,481 --> 00:28:45,110
Scott: Right in the intro of DuckTales, you do see Scrooge with the little underwater with the helmet trying to grab the treasure chest.

418
00:28:45,711 --> 00:28:48,918
Rym: There are multiple underwater arcs in every form of DuckTales.

419
00:28:49,867 --> 00:28:51,047
Rym: Otherwise, should we get into it?

420
00:28:52,310 --> 00:28:52,755
Rym: Obelus.

421
00:28:53,745 --> 00:28:57,564
Rym: We played a tabletop game, a new one that we have not played before.

422
00:28:57,665 --> 00:28:58,494
Rym: We can talk about it.

423
00:28:59,322 --> 00:29:04,329
Scott: Yeah, I mean, I saw this game on the internet, and it looked like a kind of game that we enjoy.

424
00:29:04,812 --> 00:29:09,429
Scott: A two-player game that is mostly abstract and simple in its rules.

425
00:29:11,114 --> 00:29:15,389
Scott: But, you know, the kind that might elicit complex gameplay and strategy.

426
00:29:15,591 --> 00:29:16,622
Scott: So I picked it up.

427
00:29:16,683 --> 00:29:17,127
Scott: It was cheap.

428
00:29:17,228 --> 00:29:17,854
Scott: It was tiny.

429
00:29:17,995 --> 00:29:19,570
Scott: Doesn't take up a lot of room in my apartment.

430
00:29:20,710 --> 00:29:24,320
Scott: I played it with some co-workers while I was in the other side of the country.

431
00:29:24,380 --> 00:29:26,346
Scott: I played it at PAX West with some people.

432
00:29:27,810 --> 00:29:29,449
Scott: Rym was not at either of those things.

433
00:29:30,113 --> 00:29:36,849
Scott: And then I came back to the East Coast, and eventually, I think by Halloween, I played with Rym a few times.

434
00:29:37,730 --> 00:29:39,065
Scott: It doesn't take more than a few times.

435
00:29:39,085 --> 00:29:41,326
Scott: You might think, "Oh, you're reviewing a game after you've played it a few

436
00:29:41,366 --> 00:29:41,689
Scott: times.".

437
00:29:41,790 --> 00:29:44,095
Scott: It's like, "Well, listen, we haven't mastered the

438
00:29:44,136 --> 00:29:44,217
Scott: game.".

439
00:29:44,237 --> 00:29:47,310
Rym: I mean, I've reviewed games from half a playthrough and reading the rules.

440
00:29:48,113 --> 00:29:48,416
Rym: Yeah.

441
00:29:49,043 --> 00:29:51,410
Scott: There's not much to this game, since it's so tiny.

442
00:29:52,391 --> 00:29:56,841
Scott: We can't give you the winning strategy, because I haven't played it that much.

443
00:29:57,265 --> 00:30:02,669
Scott: Some players I beat handily, and some players... One co-worker just totally crushed me.

444
00:30:04,055 --> 00:30:05,570
Scott: He figured it out in two seconds.

445
00:30:05,970 --> 00:30:06,618
Rym: It's pretty good.

446
00:30:06,881 --> 00:30:18,419
Rym: The quick version of this review is, it's the kind of game that you want to... I would keep this on me if I was at a convention, to just quickly play with another person while I'm waiting for the main event to happen, or some other game to finish.

447
00:30:19,206 --> 00:30:27,649
Scott: But I think the game only appeals to a person who would enjoy an abstract, kind of two-player competitive thing.

448
00:30:27,931 --> 00:30:36,216
Rym: It's the kids who played NIMH, which you may also know as the game where you draw a few little stick lines in a row on a piece of paper and take turns crossing them off.

449
00:30:36,539 --> 00:30:38,496
Rym: It's basically a highly evolved form of that.

450
00:30:38,517 --> 00:30:43,009
Scott: I think it's something that a chess player, you could distract them with it for a bit.

451
00:30:44,754 --> 00:30:49,627
Scott: It's like if they're waiting for the next game during a chess tournament, they'd play an obelisk or two.

452
00:30:49,850 --> 00:30:50,418
Scott: They'd be like,

453
00:30:50,438 --> 00:30:50,702
Scott: "Ooh!"

454
00:30:52,116 --> 00:30:55,910
Scott: But they wouldn't... You can't make obelisks your game and play it a whole bunch.

455
00:30:56,051 --> 00:30:56,253
Scott: No.

456
00:30:56,556 --> 00:30:57,667
Rym: It's not a lifestyle game.

457
00:30:58,731 --> 00:30:58,914
Scott: No.

458
00:30:59,036 --> 00:31:08,354
Scott: It's a two-player fillery game, a quick one, but that still gives your mind something really to think about.

459
00:31:08,516 --> 00:31:09,710
Scott: that's a bit challenging.

460
00:31:10,470 --> 00:31:13,047
Scott: But before you have to think too hard, it ends.

461
00:31:13,991 --> 00:31:28,629
Rym: So, designed by Chris A. Williams, who I was not familiar with, and looking, he's done a few other games that actually all look about the same level of depth, but Obelisk is apparently a rework of another game he designed called Dark Spires.

462
00:31:29,833 --> 00:31:33,709
Scott: I didn't realize that until today, thanks to BoardGameGeek, that Obelisk was a remake.

463
00:31:34,670 --> 00:31:41,070
Scott: I thought it was an original... The original and Obelisk are both by the same person, so it's like they remade their own game better.

464
00:31:41,790 --> 00:31:45,729
Scott: They improved their own game and printed a new version with better art and better pieces.

465
00:31:46,070 --> 00:31:47,549
Rym: Yeah, which is honestly a good way to go.

466
00:31:48,510 --> 00:31:56,617
Scott: Yeah, and I read the rules of Dark Spires, and having only played Obelisk, I can tell you, they improved upon the rules of Dark Spires.

467
00:31:56,637 --> 00:32:05,097
Scott: There's no reason to play Dark Spires other than as an educational exercise in game development of why is Obelisk better?

468
00:32:05,845 --> 00:32:06,895
Scott: Obelisk is just better.

469
00:32:06,976 --> 00:32:11,180
Scott: They made a game, they played it, they improved it, and they printed it again.

470
00:32:11,200 --> 00:32:16,632
Scott: It's Dark Spires 2.0, Dark Spires 1.0 can go die in a fire.

471
00:32:17,177 --> 00:32:19,770
Scott: It's not that much worse, but it is meaningfully worse.

472
00:32:20,131 --> 00:32:20,635
Rym: So what do you do?

473
00:32:20,736 --> 00:32:34,616
Rym: You lay eight cards in a little circle, and then you basically just take turns either putting a guy on an Obelisk or moving one of your guys from one of the Obelisks to another Obelisk.

474
00:32:35,162 --> 00:32:41,079
Scott: Now the catch is that all your guys, you only have four guys, and each guy is a d4.

475
00:32:41,827 --> 00:32:51,445
Scott: And when you want to move a guy, you can move him clockwise or counterclockwise, but he can only move the number of spaces equal to the current number on the d4.

476
00:32:52,310 --> 00:32:53,099
Rym: Yep, got a one.

477
00:32:53,119 --> 00:32:54,130
Rym: He can only move one space.

478
00:32:54,871 --> 00:32:56,493
Scott: So it can either go one left or one right.

479
00:32:56,634 --> 00:32:58,310
Scott: A two can go two left or two right.

480
00:32:58,753 --> 00:33:00,614
Scott: And a four only has one move.

481
00:33:00,695 --> 00:33:07,085
Scott: It can go straight across the board because it's an eight space circle, but it can go left or right to reach the across the board.

482
00:33:08,872 --> 00:33:13,099
Scott: Also, the four, you can only have, you can have as many ones, twos, and threes as you want.

483
00:33:13,463 --> 00:33:16,574
Scott: You can only have one four because fours are special.

484
00:33:17,100 --> 00:33:20,149
Scott: They basically can't be killed except by another four.

485
00:33:20,470 --> 00:33:21,330
Scott: They're super strong.

486
00:33:22,212 --> 00:33:23,908
Scott: And yes, pieces can be killed.

487
00:33:23,948 --> 00:33:26,917
Scott: If you land on an enemy piece, you bump it off.

488
00:33:27,402 --> 00:33:31,219
Scott: And if you land on a place where your guy has been bumped off, you can rescue them.

489
00:33:31,967 --> 00:33:37,282
Scott: But you cannot jump over an enemy piece when you move.

490
00:33:37,827 --> 00:33:39,099
Scott: So they block you in.

491
00:33:39,361 --> 00:33:44,269
Scott: You can jump over your own pieces, but you can't land on your own pieces.

492
00:33:45,114 --> 00:33:51,358
Scott: So when the board starts to fill up with more dudes, it can become difficult to move.

493
00:33:51,399 --> 00:34:03,724
Scott: It's like some pieces just won't have legal moves and, you know, you might have to take the only legal move available to you, or you might have to just bring a new guy onto the board because you can't make a legal move.

494
00:34:03,825 --> 00:34:05,139
Scott: You can only make a legal summon.

495
00:34:06,008 --> 00:34:11,178
Scott: And the game ends when one player does not have any legal moves available to them.

496
00:34:12,106 --> 00:34:13,768
Scott: They lose, game over, the other person wins.

497
00:34:14,351 --> 00:34:14,554
Rym: Yep.

498
00:34:14,574 --> 00:34:18,070
Rym: Takes about six minutes to play, once you know the rules.

499
00:34:18,431 --> 00:34:20,409
Rym: It is real quick.

500
00:34:22,286 --> 00:34:26,789
Rym: And I can say that in the course of a few plays, I can see some strategies.

501
00:34:28,592 --> 00:34:33,438
Rym: I'd say this is, like, we bring up modulus math a lot in games, and this game is a modulus math game.

502
00:34:33,478 --> 00:34:37,801
Rym: However, you don't need to actually do any modulus math because the board is so small.

503
00:34:38,123 --> 00:34:52,168
Rym: You just have to really think about the different, like, twos and fours can only interact with certain parts of the board, but threes can sort of rotate around and hit different parts of the board as they move, and ones are just sort of obvious to deal with.

504
00:34:52,654 --> 00:34:56,594
Rym: So, you can do a little bit of analysis to try to corner people.

505
00:34:58,007 --> 00:35:10,109
Rym: The game is very, I would say, strictly choreographed, and I suspect it wouldn't take a ton of play to figure out the solution, because either player one will always win or player two will always win.

506
00:35:10,894 --> 00:35:14,857
Scott: I don't know if that's true for sure, but there's no way to tie in this game.

507
00:35:14,898 --> 00:35:15,508
Rym: It has to be true.

508
00:35:16,592 --> 00:35:18,653
Scott: So, there's no way to force a tie.

509
00:35:18,754 --> 00:35:31,298
Scott: So, because there's no way to force a tie, there must be some Nash equilibrium where either the first player wins or the second player is away for the first player to force a win or the second player to force a win.

510
00:35:31,339 --> 00:35:56,290
Rym: Because obelisks get burned and can't be used for resurrections anymore as the game goes on, that is the mechanical clock that forces a conclusion, and it forces, I don't believe the game, the only chance that the game does not have a solution where either the first player or second player is guaranteed to win would be if the game can get into a state where both players can continue to play forever, but I don't know if that is possible.

511
00:35:57,416 --> 00:35:59,447
Rym: I think it could be analyzed, but I have not done so.

512
00:36:01,573 --> 00:36:07,459
Scott: I mean, I guess if nobody ever, if you could just move back and forth a bit without attacking each other.

513
00:36:07,580 --> 00:36:14,610
Rym: Yep, but the question is not can both players conspire to move in such a circular fashion that the game never ends.

514
00:36:14,893 --> 00:36:18,870
Rym: The question is, can one player force the game to a conclusion?

515
00:36:19,970 --> 00:36:20,470
Scott: Yes, exactly.

516
00:36:20,610 --> 00:36:38,854
Scott: So yeah, Rym alluded to it, but the one mechanic that I did not see in the rules of Darkspire, Darkspire had a different rule that was worse, is that whenever you start the game and one of the eight spaces has a red gem on it that basically says you cannot summon here, otherwise this is normal space.

517
00:36:38,894 --> 00:36:44,689
Scott: You can move there, right, you can rescue there, you can kill people there, but you can't summon there.

518
00:36:45,153 --> 00:36:50,324
Scott: And any time somebody is rescued, you add another red gem to another space.

519
00:36:50,485 --> 00:37:03,983
Scott: So basically after eight, seven rescues have occurred, no matter what, they're all the spaces will have red gems on them, which means no more summoning can occur.

520
00:37:04,568 --> 00:37:09,839
Scott: And if no more summoning can occur, any piece that is not on the board cannot re-enter the board.

521
00:37:09,859 --> 00:37:18,209
Scott: You'll simply have the pieces available to you, you'll be forced to move them on your turn, otherwise you lose if you can't have a legal move.

522
00:37:18,973 --> 00:37:28,449
Scott: And eventually by moving them around, somebody will eventually lose, get their last piece killed unavoidably or just be unable to move.

523
00:37:29,193 --> 00:37:42,649
Scott: Yeah, it's like could there be one a way where, you know, both players in that situation have like one piece each and just keep moving back and forth without ever putting themselves in a position where they could be killed on their turn?

524
00:37:43,351 --> 00:37:43,473
Rym: Yep.

525
00:37:43,493 --> 00:37:45,820
Rym: So that could be, I could imagine that is a state.

526
00:37:45,860 --> 00:37:47,273
Rym: if the game could get into a state where...

527
00:37:47,314 --> 00:37:48,425
Scott: Some sort of stalemate?

528
00:37:48,708 --> 00:37:58,908
Rym: Oh, the problem is, if I take a piece and it is your penultimate piece, we need the game to be in a state where we both just have a one, but then I'll be next to you.

529
00:38:00,392 --> 00:38:05,669
Rym: I guess if you had a two, you couldn't move past me and then I had a one, I'd have to do some analysis.

530
00:38:07,072 --> 00:38:09,859
Scott: I think it would be, you have two pieces left and I have one.

531
00:38:09,879 --> 00:38:12,484
Scott: I kill one of your pieces, I capture it.

532
00:38:12,646 --> 00:38:14,524
Scott: It can't come back because there's red.

533
00:38:14,545 --> 00:38:16,484
Rym: And I can't catch you and you can't catch me.

534
00:38:16,645 --> 00:38:21,293
Scott: I'm far away, my last remaining piece is far away from your last remaining piece.

535
00:38:21,313 --> 00:38:23,233
Scott: You have a small number, you can't reach me.

536
00:38:23,980 --> 00:38:32,629
Scott: And so you move a small move and then I move and we always back and forth, you have to change your number whenever you move.

537
00:38:33,275 --> 00:38:34,549
Scott: You can't stay on the same number.

538
00:38:35,113 --> 00:38:36,263
Scott: So that helps a little bit.

539
00:38:36,626 --> 00:38:39,068
Scott: But you could switch back and forth between one and two.

540
00:38:40,115 --> 00:38:40,237
Scott: Right?

541
00:38:40,278 --> 00:38:40,830
Scott: Consecutively.

542
00:38:41,030 --> 00:38:41,899
Scott: One, two, one, two.

543
00:38:42,768 --> 00:38:45,230
Scott: And just always stay far away from the other player.

544
00:38:45,590 --> 00:38:51,890
Scott: Because if you change to three, you might give the other person an opportunity to get close to you and that would be threatening.

545
00:38:52,692 --> 00:39:00,450
Rym: So we'd have to look at the modulus math of the fact that there are eight spaces and small numbers of pieces that might remain.

546
00:39:01,070 --> 00:39:12,220
Rym: But either player one always wins, player two always wins, or the question is can one player force the game to a conclusion?

547
00:39:12,240 --> 00:39:19,027
Rym: Or can one player prevent the game from ending no matter what the other player attempts to do?

548
00:39:20,671 --> 00:39:27,010
Rym: Because if I can prevent the game from ever ending, the game will be a draw and last forever and that is a possible outcome.

549
00:39:28,611 --> 00:39:36,600
Scott: And still, if you like other small, abstract two player strategy games that don't take a long time and don't take up a lot of space.

550
00:39:37,145 --> 00:39:39,130
Scott: So you want to play chess in two seconds.

551
00:39:39,690 --> 00:39:41,009
Scott: This is even smaller than the Duke.

552
00:39:42,293 --> 00:39:43,248
Scott: It's smaller than oshi.

553
00:39:47,162 --> 00:39:52,277
Scott: you can play a random mode where you roll the dice or you can play the pure mode where you don't roll the dice.

554
00:39:52,418 --> 00:39:53,994
Rym: Play the pure mode, it's just better.

555
00:39:54,014 --> 00:39:57,987
Scott: Yeah, but some people might want to mix it up with a little randomness.

556
00:39:59,470 --> 00:40:00,585
Scott: It's just what they want to do.

557
00:40:02,699 --> 00:40:07,594
Scott: But yeah, the pure mode is the only mode that really, you know...

558
00:40:08,551 --> 00:40:09,781
Rym: Yeah, but it's a pretty good game.

559
00:40:09,801 --> 00:40:19,496
Rym: Like if you didn't own it, I'd probably pick up a copy and I imagine you'll have it in your messenger bag when we're walking around PAX and we'll play it while we're waiting for some of their game to finish.

560
00:40:20,547 --> 00:40:24,687
Scott: And if you want to buy it, you will go to phaseshiftgames.com.

561
00:40:25,632 --> 00:40:27,249
Scott: I believe is the publisher.

562
00:40:28,570 --> 00:40:33,210
Scott: And is it actually... I don't know if they have copies right now.

563
00:40:33,330 --> 00:40:35,171
Scott: Oh no, the MSRP is $20.

564
00:40:35,171 --> 00:40:38,285
Scott: But I don't know if you can actually get one or not.

565
00:40:38,427 --> 00:40:39,706
Scott: I preordered it.

566
00:40:41,230 --> 00:40:43,948
Scott: Oh no, they have it for... Do they?

567
00:40:45,011 --> 00:40:46,269
Scott: I don't know if you can buy it right now.

568
00:40:47,092 --> 00:40:54,629
Rym: One industry that's going to be hit exceedingly and exceptionally hard by these ridiculous tariffs is going to be the tabletop industry.

569
00:40:55,450 --> 00:40:58,288
Rym: So, if there's any board game you want, buy it now.

570
00:40:59,393 --> 00:41:00,369
Scott: Get a digital version?

571
00:41:01,890 --> 00:41:03,936
Scott: Or, just buy a bunch of game components.

572
00:41:04,947 --> 00:41:05,735
Scott: Like a whole bunch of them.

573
00:41:05,755 --> 00:41:06,522
Scott: I already have a bunch.

574
00:41:06,664 --> 00:41:09,455
Scott: But then you can just play any game, just make it with pieces.

575
00:41:09,475 --> 00:41:12,348
Rym: I feel like we spend a lot of time on board game arena in the time to come.

576
00:41:13,532 --> 00:41:13,875
Scott: We'll see.

577
00:41:14,098 --> 00:41:16,243
Scott: I mean, I won't buy that many games anyway.

578
00:41:16,487 --> 00:41:16,669
Rym: True.

579
00:41:17,351 --> 00:41:19,250
Rym: In fact, I actually want to go back into the old library.

580
00:41:19,350 --> 00:41:20,569
Rym: I want to play La Chita again.

581
00:41:22,334 --> 00:41:25,010
Rym: I saw it up there and I suddenly remembered that I like that game.

582
00:41:26,238 --> 00:41:26,750
Scott: Yeah, it's okay.

583
00:41:26,970 --> 00:41:27,757
Scott: Politics cards.

584
00:41:27,959 --> 00:41:29,229
Rym: Yeah, I've only played it a few times.

585
00:41:30,171 --> 00:41:33,938
Rym: I brought it to a magfest right before the pandemic and then we didn't play it the whole time.

586
00:41:33,958 --> 00:41:35,110
Rym: And then I brought it home.

587
00:41:40,120 --> 00:41:42,471
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

588
00:41:42,532 --> 00:41:45,040
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

589
